4. COMPENSATION AND THE SHAPLEY BASIS

Commercial terms must specify the licensed scope, term, fees or computable
payment mechanism, payment dates, reporting, confidentiality, and the process
for resolving valuation and payment disputes. Material expansions of scope
require the agreement's applicable expansion terms or a further agreement.

The Licensor's compensation policy is substantial return proportionate to
attributable value, not merely the cost of copying or replacing the artifact.
A commercial agreement may implement this through an agreed fixed fee,
minimum guarantee, milestones, royalties, or retrospective value-sharing.
If compensation depends on realized value, the agreement must define the
value function, contributor set, attribution method, valuation period,
allowable costs, reporting evidence, and settlement procedure in advance.
No undefined “fair share” is substituted for those payment terms.

For contributions N and a stated value function v, the Shapley value of i is:

    phi_i(v) = sum over S subset of N excluding i:
      [|S|! (|N|-|S|-1)! / |N|!] * [v(S union {i}) - v(S)]

This averages marginal contribution over coalition orders. One leave-one-out
comparison, replacement cost, or an invented precision score is not an exact
Shapley value. Account for complementary contributors, feasible coalitions,
relevant beneficiaries, adverse effects, and substitutes actually available
at the time of need. Do not manipulate the share by splitting one interest
into nominal players or hiding upstream work inside a downstream player.

Use explicitly described approximations and uncertainty ranges where exact
computation is impractical. Do not turn survival, dignity, or incomparable
public benefits into an invented monetary invoice. Shapley allocation depends
on the chosen game; it is neither an automatic debt nor uniquely determined
moral desert. The executed agreement, not a unilateral valuation, determines
the payment obligation. This license promises no particular recovery amount.

5. ATTRIBUTION, REDISTRIBUTION, AND PROVENANCE

Keep this license and applicable copyright and source notices with copies or
substantial portions of the Work. Mark material modifications and preserve
the distinction between recovered source material and machine-grown work.
Do not represent attribution as endorsement.

When redistributing protected portions of the Work or modifications of them,
pass on this license and do not purport to grant commercial rights you do not
hold. Recipients obtain the no-fee permissions directly from the Licensor and
must independently obtain any required commercial permission. This condition
does not extend the license to separable independent work or alter a third
party's license. Charging for redistribution requires commercial permission.

6. THE HARVEST ACCOUNTING

For use relying on Section 2, the Licensor may directly request a good-faith
account of the Work's use, realized benefits, costs, and attributable value
at most once in twelve months and never in the first 180 days of that use.
A request must identify the Work, reporting period, Licensor, and a working
reply address. Respond within 60 days after actual delivery.

Report what is reasonably known; identify uncertainty and explain when a
quantitative estimate is infeasible. No fabricated number, factorial
computation, new experiment, registration, telemetry, disclosure of personal
data, privileged material, trade secrets, or unlawful disclosure is required.
An honest zero-value account is allowed for Section 2 use. It does not cure
unlicensed commercial use or cancel payment owed under a commercial agreement.

Individual accounts and confidential annexes may be published only with the
provider's consent. An organization must provide a public-safe summary that
the Licensor may attribute and publish, excluding personal data, trade
secrets, and privileged material. The Licensor must redact third-party
personal data before publication. Commercial agreements may replace this
section with agreed confidential reporting.

7. BREACH AND CURE

Commercial use without the separate permission required by Section 3 is
outside this grant from the outset. Stopping that use does not retroactively
license it. The Licensor reserves available legal remedies for infringement
and breach of an executed commercial agreement; no damages waiver or automatic
zero-payment cure applies to unauthorized commercial exploitation.

For a failure to satisfy Sections 5 or 6, the Licensor must give written
notice identifying the specific deficiency and allow 30 days after delivery
to cure before suspending the affected user's Section 2 permissions. Cure
restores those permissions. An honestly explained valuation disagreement is
not itself a breach. Suspension does not cancel independent prior grants or
compliant downstream users' permissions. Commercial agreements govern their
own termination and cure terms.

